column90:
Expressiveness usually means "working code to boilerplate ratio". For example: (C#)<p><pre><code>    SomeMethod((x) => SomeOtherMethod(x));
</code></pre>
is more expressive than: (java)<p><pre><code>    class WrapperIDontReallyNeed() {
        public void AnotherWrapper(uselessType x) {
            SomeOtherMethod(x);
        }
    }
    ...
    SomeMethod(new WrapperIDontReallyNeed());
</code></pre>
(or anonymous classes - before someone points out they exist - just trading Wrapper for IWrapper and no `class` keyword)<p>And again - I don't see any mention from his side about being powerful. I don't think he's confusing anything either - expressiveness of a language is a pretty common term and is true in this case. C++ is easy to beat from that point of view, unless you add a tonne of Boost.
